Well, I just lost another average game that wasn't headed in any particular
direction to your ordinary, average wererat.  Not altogether exciting or
frustrating (I had an uncursed ring of levitation, but levels 1-4 had no
altars nor shops to speak of, nor had I found any armor or weapons, a
necessity for starting archeologists), except for the way I died.

See, I'd had a short fight with a gnome lord wielding a single-edged
polearm, and needless to say I wasn't winning.  So I retreated up a level
to rest, sat where I wasn't likely to be ganged up upon and rested for
50 turns.  About ten turns into it, I see a:
"The wererat changes into a ratwere --MORE--
"The ratwere bites! --MORE--
"You die. --MORE--

Now, tell me, where in the manual of archeologists skills does it say,
"When encountering a wererat, let it hit you once before reacting.",
may I ask?  Had I been in the middle of the room, I would have been
susceptible to swarms of anything, and, besides, while ^A works on
my machine, it doesn't on the Unix machines (for stopping doing things),
so one can hardly hold that as an excuse.  (I always wondered why
^A was used, seeing as it's also the "do again" key; you'd think a
separate key would help avoid accidentally restarting what one meant
to stop.)

So what are we supposed to do about this?  If I wear the ring of
levitation (which I don't generally have anyway) I consume extra
food.  If I enscribe Elbereth, it'll fade away soonish.  There's
really no acceptable way of preventing the unrealistic.

(And, of course, this happens with anything, not just a wererat.)

I give this two thumbs down: unrealistic, and unplayable.
You should either stop as soon as you see a monster move (my choice)
or *before* it gets a chance to hit you, at the very least.
Oh, and I'd also appreciate, at least as an option, the proposed
somewhat more responsive user-interface, whereby there'd be a "command
line" somewhere on the screen-- a "> ", and the command ("move left",
"run left", "Run left," "'port", "50 rest", etc.) would be displayed
until it was finished.  If interrupted, "You stop resting after <xx>
turns" might be called for.

Will I do the coding for this?  No, that would imply that I'm not a lazy
slob who doesn't want other people to write his game features for him,
which isn't the case.

Still a great game.  Hat's off to the dev team.


